            <ArticleTitle>Effects of a new device to guide venous puncture in elderly critically ill patients: results of a pilot randomized study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>335-339</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s40520-016-0547-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Novel devices based on the emission of near-infrared electromagnetic radiation (NIR) have been developed to minimize venous puncture failures. These instruments produce an &quot;augmented reality&quot; image, in which subcutaneous veins are depicted on a LCD display. We compared the new technique with standard venipuncture in a population of elderly patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients admitted in Intensive Care Unit were randomized to standard or to NIR assisted procedure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In the 103 enrolled patients (age 74 ± 12 years; standard venipuncture-N = 56; NIR-N = 47), no differences were found in procedure length, number of attempts, and referred pain. With NIR there was a lower incidence of hematomas and fewer anxiety and depressive symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of the novel NIR-based device is safer and more psychologically tolerable, and it is not associated to an increase of procedure length or number of attempts.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
